{"id": "TASK-20261018-DIR-PW-NAV-CENTER", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Resolve 'icitywork' to the DIR Public Works Portal and pull the Navigation Center public-works registration", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records)", "Law 1 (repeated processes become tools)"], "result": "User identified 'icitywork' as the HB public works portal. Local artifacts located: C:\\Users\\Amd949609\\Downloads\\'Contractors - Public Works Portal.html', '2Project - Public Works Portal.html' and x_cdoi2_csm_portal_project.csv (137,567,384 bytes, 654,284 rows, SHA-256 392d6a56315be19308d3a073ec92ef16b1b37b1497e84628fa9214c862f9f717) - all California DIR (services.dir.ca.gov) ServiceNow portal captures. iCityWork.com is an unrelated vendor (Project Explorer); its public tenant API (/api/public/tenants) lists only Dublin, Lafayette, Pleasant Hill and Demo - no Huntington Beach tenant. DIR registry row 20240180643: awarding_body 'City of Huntington Beach', name 'Huntington Beach Navigation Center', description 'Construction of Emergency Homeless Shelter', contract CC 1597, DIR project ID 346300, status Approved, start 2020-09-16, end 2020-12-30. This is an independent state filing confirming the shelter build as a registered public works project (Labor Code 1720 et seq.: prevailing wage, apprenticeship, PWCR, certified payroll 1776). Federal exposure: Legistar 20-1799 (8/3/2020) authorized up to $2,220,000 of site work / FF&E using COVID-19 response funds and a $2,596,240 Mercy House operator contract (CDBG, SB2, COVID funds). Contractor and award amount are absent from the CSV export; anonymous project-detail API returns HTTP 401 and the eCPR search widget is reCAPTCHA-gated (isGuest=true, captcha property sn_ext_usr_reg.captchaEnabled=true).", "tool": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\dir_public_works_extract.py; C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\cdp_fetch.py", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\dir_public_works\\DIR_PW_NAV_CENTER_20261018; C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\investigation_dossiers\\DIR_PUBLIC_WORKS_NAV_CENTER_20261018.md", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T14:17:08.446105Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-FEMA-CONTRACT-MATRIX", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Extract the Navigation Center professional-services contract matrix and the FEMA reimbursement link", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records)"], "result": "Legistar 21-256 (passed 2021-04-19) attachments Att#1 Q3-2020 and Att#2 Q4-2020 recovered and parsed. Shelter build team: RPM Team LLC (construction design management, $27,680 -> $39,400), Totum Consulting (construction project management, $143,000 -> $304,050 in four amendments), EEC Environmental (environmental construction oversight for 17631 Cameron Ln and 17642 Beach Blvd, $63,000 -> $138,000), AESCO (testing and inspection for construction of the new shelter facility, $55,100 -> $66,800). RPM, Totum and AESCO amendments dated 2020-08-27 / 2020-09-30 / 2020-10-19 state they were made 'to comply with FEMA Reimbursement Requests' - i.e. the shelter construction is FEMA-reimbursed federal money, which pairs with the DIR public-works registration (CC 1597) to support a federal prevailing-wage / procurement compliance inquiry and, if wages or payroll were misstated, an FCA theory. New address variant found: 2020-12-22 EEC amendment writes '7642 Beach Blvd' (fourth variant). Logged as OBSTR-HBNC-ADDRESS-2026-10-18-07, intent UNVERIFIED.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\dir_public_works\\DIR_PW_NAV_CENTER_20261018\\CITY_CONTRACT_LISTS", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T14:21:00.279168Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-FEMA-PA-FEDERAL-AWARD-CHAIN", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Resolve the FEMA Public Assistance applicant record for the City of Huntington Beach and enumerate every dollar FEMA obligated for the Navigation Center", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "OpenFEMA public open-data API resolved the City of Huntington Beach to FEMA applicant number 059-36000-00 (2000 Main Street) with five disaster numbers: 1577, 1585, 1952, 4305 and 4482 (COVID-19, declared 2020-03-22). DR-4482 total federal obligated to the City is $4,258,379.06 across 25 projects. The ONLY non-congregate-sheltering project covering the Navigation Center's actual operating year is PW 1367 'NCS Non-Congregate Sheltering ... 3-15-2020 to 1-31-2021' at $11,186.72, while PW 5299 (08/2021-01/2022) is $655,294.40 and PW 5493 (02-06/2022) is $634,488.22 - a roughly 1.8% reimbursement rate for the year the shelter operated. THREE DE-OBLIGATIONS FOUND: PW 5493 obligated +$634,488.22 on 2025-04-02 and fully reversed -$634,488.22 on 2025-04-19 (17 days; project now $0, 'Pending Closeout Award'); PW 5299 reduced -$1,711.86 on 2025-09-19; PW 5942 Management Costs reduced -$10,359.61 on 2025-10-04. Force Account Labor PWs 2307/2345/2327/2312/2335 total $1,682,117.91 of payroll-derived federal money (the reconciliation surface for any wage claim). No CFDA 97.036 line appears in the City's FY2021 SEFA. Logged as OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-09, intent UNVERIFIED. New reusable tool: tools/records_portal/openfema_pa_extract.py.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\openfema", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:05:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-CITY-FINANCIAL-RECONCILIATION", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Reconcile the Navigation Center funding across the City's audited financial statements, Single Audits and adopted budgets", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "Located and captured every financial publication on the City's own finance page (discovered via sitemap.xml, served from cms3.revize.com): Single Audit Reports FY2019-FY2022 (containing the Schedule of Expenditures of Federal Awards) and Annual Comprehensive Financial Reports FY2020-FY2022 plus adopted budgets FY2020-21 and FY2021-22. Established: (1) property acquisition $5,863,000 paid from the LOW AND MODERATE INCOME HOUSING ASSET FUND (LMIHAF, the former RDA housing fund) - ACFR FY2021 MD&A; (2) construction $5,610,000 booked in the Grants Special Revenue Fund and described in audited statements as 'anticipated to be reimbursed with FEMA Public Assistance Disaster Relief funds' - ACFR FY2021 and FY2022; (3) the SAME construction described in the adopted FY2021/22 budget (p.342) as built with CARES Act grants received from the State and County: 'Several million dollars in CARES grants ... were used to provide small business grants ... and to build a temporary Homeless Shelter in partnership with the County' - within $12,490,383 of FY2020/21 revised expenditure in funds 238/244/245/247; (4) total CARES Act funding received $14.3 million. FY2021 SEFA totals $18,380,604 of federal expenditure, of which $12,742,004 is CFDA 21.019 Coronavirus Relief Fund - passed through County of Orange as $4,747,522 (Small Business Relief Program), $3,772,006 (agreement MA-042-21010730) and $1,734,455, plus $2,488,021 passed through the State of California - and NO CFDA 97.036 line at all, even though CRF and FEMA PA may not fund the same cost (2 CFR 200.405, 200.425). FY2022 ACFR: Grants Special Revenue Fund deficit 'due to expenditures incurred in response to the COVID-19 pandemic that are currently under review for reimbursement by FEMA'. Logged as OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-08 (DUPLICATION-OF-BENEFIT RISK, intent UNVERIFIED).", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\city_docs", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:06:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-EFAU-FEDERAL-TERMS", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Extract the federal compliance chain from the executed Emergency Facility Use Agreement and the signed Letter of Intent, and OCR the image-only legislative scans", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "The Emergency Facility Use Agreement (Legistar 20-1578, executed 2020-04-20) between the County of Orange (as 'Licensee') and the City of Huntington Beach (as 'City'), for a 'temporary alternative shelter site (TASS)' at 17631 Cameron Lane, imports the full federal chain into one instrument: 44 CFR 206.326 and 2 CFR Pt.2 App.II (cl.7.a); Copeland Anti-Kickback Act 18 USC 874 / 29 CFR Pt.3 (cl.VII); DAVIS-BACON ACT COMPLIANCE requiring payment of not less than the greater of the federal or California DIR wage rates, citing http://www.dir.ca.gov/lcp.asp, applicable to construction contracts over $2,000 (cl.VIII); Contract Work Hours and Safety Standards Act 40 USC 327-330 / 29 CFR Pt.5 with 1.5x overtime, $10/day liquidated damages and County withholding authority, flowed down to all subcontracts (cl.IX); 2 CFR 200.321 MBE/WBE (cl.XVIII); 2 CFR 200.322 (cl.XIX); and the Uniform Administrative Requirements (cl.XX). Clause XVIII states 'The County intends to seek reimbursement of its costs incurred in connection with this project from FEMA', and Clause 8.a-b obliges the County to reimburse the City for all direct costs. Cl.7.a makes the agreement 'conditional and contingent on incorporation of FEMA-required terms and conditions'. TWO DOCUMENTARY DEFECTS: Attachment D2 'Certification Regarding Lobbying' (31 USC 1352, described in its own text as 'a material representation of fact upon which reliance was placed', penalties $10,000-$100,000 per failure) carries no signature and no date - logged OBSTR-HBNC-CONTRACT-2026-10-18-10; and Davis-Bacon cl.VIII.B instructs the wage-determination lookup to select 'Sonoma' County in an Orange County project 450 miles away - logged OBSTR-HBNC-CONTRACT-2026-10-18-11. Also OCR'd the signed Letter of Intent of 2020-01-16 (seller Mitsuru Yamada, Trustee of the Mitsuru Yamada Living Trust U/D/T dated 1998-09-17; 17631 Cameron Ln APN 167-472-08 at $3,077,010; option on 17642 Beach Blvd APN 167-472-09 at $3,089,250, option expiry 2021-07-31; combined $6,166,260) which discloses that CBRE represented BOTH buyer and seller and that 'Buyer and Seller each agree to pay the half of the brokerage commissions'; and the Acquisition and Sale Agreement effective 2020-09-09 between the Huntington Beach City School District and the City for three 24x40 portables from Sowers Middle School, considered in-kind and expressly installed 'as part of a homeless shelter to be constructed on City's property located at 17631 Cameron Lane' - with the district's recitals stating the portables are surplus partly because they are 'not being certified by DSA'. The same 2020-02-18 agenda file contains a public comment asserting the LOI predated the overlay change ('you have already decided before the public meeting') and the City's Historic Resources Board reviewer stating the 1947 structure at 17631 Cameron Lane 'was listed as eligible for Cal Register status in both of the past historic surveys' but 'CR status sounds a lot more important than it actually is'. Fifth address variant found: '17881 Beach Blvd' in the 19-1009 communications file - logged OBSTR-HBNC-ADDRESS-2026-10-18-12.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\LEGISTAR_RESO_2019-22_20261018\\attachments\\20-1578 and C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\legistar", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:07:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-FAC-SINGLE-AUDIT-SEFA", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Resolve the CRF-vs-FEMA duplication question using the Federal Audit Clearinghouse five-year SEFA series for the City of Huntington Beach", "laws": ["Law 7 (complaint/issue = immediate end-to-end fix)", "Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "The City's own website publishes Single Audits only through FY2022, and the FY2023/FY2024 ACFRs mention FEMA ZERO times - so the FEMA Public Assistance reconciliation could not be completed from City sources. Located the GSA Federal Audit Clearinghouse (FAC) PUBLIC bulk dissemination data at https://app.fac.gov/dissemination/public-data/gsa/full/{general.csv (~273 MB), federal_awards.csv (1.35 GB), findings.csv, findings_text.csv, corrective_action_plans.csv, notes_to_sefa.csv, passthrough.csv} - no API key, no login, no request. Resolved auditee: City of Huntington Beach, EIN 956000723, UEI LKKFRC4PW7L4, auditor Davis Farr LLP; reports AY2021 2021-06-CENSUS-0000132162 ($18,380,604), AY2022 2022-06-CENSUS-0000132162 ($6,713,604), AY2023 2023-06-GSAFAC-0000035634 ($35,046,517), AY2024 2024-06-GSAFAC-0000362040 ($10,044,047), AY2025 2025-06-GSAFAC-0000414152 ($11,242,906). Streamed the 1.35 GB federal_awards.csv and extracted 144 SEFA rows for the City across five audit years. DECISIVE RESULT: CFDA 97.036 (FEMA Public Assistance) by audit year = AY2021 $0 (absent entirely) / AY2022 $69,452 / AY2023 $148,917 / AY2024 $3,340,898 / AY2025 $847,989, total $4,407,256 - which reconciles to FEMA's own OpenFEMA obligation ledger for DR-4482 ($4,258,379.06), and is ZERO in FY2021, the very year the $5,610,000 Navigation Center construction was booked in the Grants Special Revenue Fund as 'anticipated to be reimbursed with FEMA Public Assistance Disaster Relief funds'. THREE CROSS-VALIDATIONS: (1) $4,407,256 recognized vs $4,258,379.06 obligated, two independent federal datasets agreeing; (2) AY2022 97.036 $69,452 equals OpenFEMA PW 1214 'Personal Protective Equipment-Public Safety' $69,452.21 obligated 2021-12-01 TO THE CENT; (3) the FAC-derived AY2021 SEFA total $18,380,604 reconciles EXACTLY to the City's own published FY2021 Single Audit PDF already in the locker, validating the extraction. CONCLUSION: the duplication-of-benefit concern raised in OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-08 is NOT SUPPORTED - FEMA never reimbursed the construction; no double recovery occurred. What survives is a disclosure-quality finding about over-optimistic revenue recognition in FY2021/FY2022 audited statements (the Grants fund deficit was recovered not by FEMA but by an unrelated $4,900,000 AEG Developer Funds transfer, FY2023 equity $655,000, FY2024 $2,712,000). Logged as OBSTR-HBNC-FUNDING-2026-10-18-13; OBSTR -08 marked RESOLVED NEGATIVE and retained unedited; dossier section 4A added and the misleading line in section 8 struck through. NOT AFFECTED: the Davis-Bacon/Copeland/Contract Work Hours chain (entries -10, -11), DIR project 20240180643 / contract CC 1597, and the $1,682,117.91 of FEMA Force Account Labor - the wage question remains OPEN and still turns on certified payroll for CC 1597. Related gain: AY2023 SEFA shows 14.218 COVID-19 CARES Act/CV $1,628,957 and 21.027 CSLFRF $29,606,925. New reusable tool: tools/records_portal/fac_single_audit_extract.py. Remaining sub-target: FAC findings.csv / findings_text.csv / notes_to_sefa.csv for the City's five audit years.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\fac_single_audit", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T19:30:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-CDGB-CV-CAPER-FUNDING-MAP", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Close out the complete funding map for the Cameron Lane Navigation Center using the City's HUD CAPER and Annual Action Plan filings", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "Discovered the City's entire HUD housing-document library through sitemap/City page enumeration and captured 21 documents (Con Plan, Annual Action Plans 2019-20 through 2024-25 with amendments, and CAPERs FY2019-20 through FY2025-26). DECISIVE FINDING - CDBG-CV, the City's own words: FY2020/21 CAPER p.3 states 'the City had a total of $2,159,775 in CARES Act funding (CDBG-CV) which, sans CDBG-CV Program Administration, was ENTIRELY ALLOCATED TO THE CAMERON LANE NAVIGATION CENTER OPERATIONS PROJECT. The Cameron Lane Navigation Center is an emergency homeless shelter located at 17631 Cameron Lane, Huntington Beach and served to protect homeless persons from contracting COVID-19...'. FY2022/23 CAPER p.1: '$2,028,847 to the Navigation Center with only $130,928 allocated to program administration... 893 homeless persons.' FY2023/24 CAPER p.1: 'all CDBG-CV funds have been expended... served 1,169 people since the pandemic started... 408 individuals, including 20 Veterans.' CDBG-CV came in two rounds: Round 1 $727,819 (FY2019/20 AAP Amendment No.2 'CARES Act Amendment') and Round 3 $1,431,956 notified 2020-09-11 (Amendment No.4 'CARES Act Amendment 2'), cumulative $2,159,775 - matching the HUD award B-20-MW-06-0506 reported to USAspending to the dollar. Amendment No.2 states in terms that the money funds 'the public service operations of an emergency homeless shelter located at 17631 Cameron Lane'. CRITICAL: CDBG-CV is a public-service grant that funded OPERATIONS ONLY and could not have paid for construction - this is the SECOND independent confirmation (after the FAC SEFA in TASK-20261018-FAC-SINGLE-AUDIT-SEFA) that the construction money was CRF/General-fund sourced, not CDBG-CV and not FEMA. COMPLETE FUNDING MAP now established: acquisition $5,863,000 LMIHAF; construction $5,610,000 Grants SRF (CARES/CRF); shelter operations 2020-2023 $2,028,847 CDBG-CV + $130,928 admin; supportive services $2,190,675 HOME-ARP; operating subsidy $3,290,000 SB2 over 5 years; FEMA PA (all DR-4482 programmes) $4,407,256 recognised. ALSO FOUND: HOME-ARP pairs the Navigation Center with 'the City's Project Homekey development, HB Oasis Project' - a SECOND Homekey project beyond the 17251 Beach Blvd motel application in Legistar 21-940. NEW RECORD-INTEGRITY FINDING (OBSTR-HBNC-NAME-2026-10-18-14): three DISTINCT facilities are all recorded as 'the Navigation Center' - 15161 Jackson Street SRO (7 units, HUD DH-2), 15311 Pipeline Lane emergency shelter (awarded $499,275 of CDBG in FY2018/19, 'on hold pending litigation', later sold 'resulting in a deficit of approximately $60,000'), and 17631 Cameron Lane. An analyst joining the documents could mis-attribute the $499,275 to the Cameron Lane site. CONTRACTS CAPTURED AND OCR'd: AESCO on-call materials testing/inspection - original agreement 2019-11-19 sum $250,000, Amendment No.1 (Legistar 19-1125, approved 2019-12-16) added $350,000 for a new not-to-exceed $600,000; and the 2021-12-21 on-call award (Legistar 21-845) where AESCO ranked #1 of 25 vendors. AESCO's contract is a unit-price on-call instrument, NOT site-specific, so the Cameron Lane testing ran through task orders and the contract alone does not price the shelter work. EEC Environmental (Environmental Engineering Contracting, Inc.): original agreement 2017-02-06, Amendment No.1 (Legistar 19-1189) extended term to 2021-02-06 with compensation not to exceed $375,000, followed by a new 3-year as-needed environmental engineering contract approved 2021-02-01 (Legistar 21-061) at $600,000 - the chain that carried EEC through the shelter period. EEC contract and both amendments OCR'd from image-only scans. Dossier gains section 4B (complete funding map + verbatim CAPER quotations + the naming-collision table); package now 100 files / 209 MB, hashed and mirrored x3 with verification.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\city_docs\\caper and ...\\contracts", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T20:15:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-PERKS-ANDROID-TERMIUS", "title": "Phone-to-cloud SSH via Termius on Samsung A16 (GH-009)", "status": "COMPLETE", "completed_at": "2026-10-18T12:45:00", "details": "Termius installed via Play Store UI automation; vault password set (recorded in Amd949609_keys/termius_vault_password.env); ed25519 key imported to Keychain; hosts AZ-VM-01 (20.127.235.165) and AZ-VM-02 (20.102.104.91), azureuser, port 22, key auth. Both fingerprints verified against live ssh-keyscan. Both shells confirmed live on phone.", "artifacts": ["C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\Amd949609_keys\\termius_azvm_ed25519", "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\Amd949609_keys\\termius_vault_password.env", "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\summaries\\TERMIUS_ANDROID_REMOTE_ACCESS_SETUP.md"], "category": "student_perks_remote_access"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-FAC-FINDINGS-NOTES", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Extract the FAC auditor findings and Notes to the SEFA for every Huntington Beach auditee, and establish whether any auditor ever flagged the shelter's federal funding", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "Extended tools/records_portal/fac_single_audit_extract.py with a --findings mode that streams findings.csv, findings_text.csv, corrective_action_plans.csv and notes_to_sefa.csv from the GSA Federal Audit Clearinghouse public bulk set and filters on the resolved report_ids (reusing the cached audits.json so the 273 MB general.csv is not re-streamed). Result: 7 findings rows, 2 findings_text rows, 2 corrective_action_plans rows, 42 notes_to_sefa rows. DECISIVE NEGATIVE: not one finding belongs to the City of Huntington Beach. All 7 findings rows carry report_ids 2016-06-CENSUS-0000134036 and 2017-06-CENSUS-0000134037, which resolve by name in audits.json to the HUNTINGTON BEACH CITY SCHOOL DISTRICT and the HUNTINGTON BEACH UNION HIGH SCHOOL DISTRICT - separate auditees swept in by the substring match 'Huntington Beach'. The City has ZERO audit findings in the FAC for AY2016 through AY2025, all ten of its reports. The two findings_text rows resolve to the literal placeholder 'GSA_MIGRATION', i.e. the narrative was never migrated into the bulk data - so the school districts' findings are unreadable anyway. The City's own Notes to the SEFA are substantive and uneventful: basis-of-presentation and accounting-policy notes; a repeated statement that the City did not use the 10% de minimis indirect cost rate; DOJ/OES pass-through detail (Violence Against Women Formula Grant LE21056860, FY2023 through FY2025); and CDBG revolving-loan balances of $1,820,732 (AY2019) and $326,414 (AY2020). Significance: the ABSENCE of a finding is what makes the funding questions interesting - across the five audit years in which the City received $4.26M of FEMA PA, ran a $5.61M construction booked as 'anticipated to be reimbursed with FEMA', expended $2.16M of CDBG-CV and recognised $29.6M of CSLFRF, no independent auditor issued a finding, questioned any cost, or reported a material weakness. Stated exactly: the audits are NOT evidence of wrongdoing and are equally NOT exculpatory - they simply did not test the question. Also settled: there is NO separate 'Huntington Beach Housing Authority' auditee in the FAC. The Authority does not file its own Single Audit; its federal money is audited inside the City's package, consistent with HBHA being a City component unit, and meaning the $5,863,000 LMIHAF acquisition sits inside the City's audit scope. Auditor for the City's reports: Davis Farr LLP.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\fac_single_audit", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T21:05:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-ACQUISITION-CHAIN-CORRECTED", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Reconstruct the 17631 Cameron Lane acquisition chain from the City's legislative record and correct the City-vs-Housing-Authority framing", "laws": ["Law 7 (complaint/issue = immediate end-to-end fix)", "Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "Pulled the full MatterTitle text (not the truncated display title) for every candidate matter via webapi.legistar.com and reconstructed the chain: 19-394 (2019-04-01) three-year lease for 5770 Research Drive + shelter-crisis action; 19-436 (2019-04-15) homelessness presentation; 19-455 Resolution 2019-22 shelter crisis (SB 850); 19-1009 (2019-10-07) direction to explore other Navigation Center options; 20-1384/20-1386 (2020-02-03) CLOSED SESSION under Gov. Code 54956.8 with City Manager Oliver Chi as negotiator; 20-1432 (2020-02-18) Council authorises the PSA and option and the fund allocation; 20-1560 (2020-04-06) HBHA Resolution No. 6; 20-1578 (2020-04-20) Emergency Facility Use Agreement; 20-1799 (2020-08-03) begin operating; 20-1875 (2020-09-21) School District portables; 20-1988 (2020-11-16) ribbon cutting. THREE CORRECTIONS: (1) Matter 20-1875 is NOT a City-HBHA instrument - the full title is 'Approve the Acquisition and Sale Agreement between the City of Huntington Beach and the HUNTINGTON BEACH CITY SCHOOL DISTRICT for the acquisition of three portable buildings for use at the City's Homeless Navigation Center'. The previously captured attachment (effective 2020-09-09, three 24x40 portables from Sowers Middle School) is therefore the CORRECT AND COMPLETE instrument for that matter; nothing is missing, and TASK-20261018-Q6-HBHA-ACQUISITION is closed on that basis. The old 'missing City-HBHA instrument' was an artefact of a truncated display title. (2) The buyer named in authorising matter 20-1432 is the CITY OF HUNTINGTON BEACH, not the Authority, and the resolution names TWO grantor trusts - 'Shigeru Yamada, Trustee of the Shigeru Yamada Living Trust, and Mitsuru Yamada, Trustee of the Mitsuru Yamada Living Trust' - whereas the Signed Letter of Intent names only Mitsuru Yamada. So the LOI and the authorising action are not the same instrument and do not name the same grantors. Yet the recorded deed for APN 167-472-08 vests in the Huntington Beach Housing Authority. THE INSTRUMENT THAT BRIDGES THOSE TWO FACTS IS STILL NOT LOCATED - a City-to-HBHA transfer, a novation, a nominee designation, or a deed executed direct to the Authority. This is now the sharpest open question in the acquisition chain and supersedes the incorrect framing. (3) HBHA Resolution No. 6 (legistar2/3534_4827_HBHA_Reso_No_6.pdf, OCR'd) is a MECHANISM, NOT AN AUTHORISATION: it simply authorises the Authority Clerk to accept and consent to deeds, reciting Gov. Code 27281 (a deed to a public agency cannot be recorded without the grantee's consent by resolution) and Health & Safety Code 34200-34380 and 34315. It contains NO property description, NO APN, NO price and NO seller. It supplies the RECORDING CONSENT, not the purchase authority. ADDITIONAL FINDINGS: the '17361 Cameron Lane' address variant is authored by the CITY ITSELF - Legistar matter 20-1432 attachment 4591 is published as '17361 Cameron Lane PowerPoint' (folded into OBSTR -12); the 2020-02-03 closed-session text characterises an ACQUISITION as a 'disposition' and the subject as 'a portion of 17631 Cameron Lane' while the purchase took two whole parcels (logged as OBSTR-HBNC-SESSION-2026-10-18-15); two Legistar attachment index entries return HTTP 404 from the City's own CDN (MatterAttachmentIds 9314 and 9521, logged as OBSTR-HBNC-AVAILABILITY-2026-10-18-16 - both are duplicates of files that survive on sibling matters); and 'Cameron Lane Townhomes' is a DIFFERENT property at 17532 Cameron Lane (TTM 19154 / CUP 21-006, 18 townhomes, CEQA Notice of Exemption under Guidelines 15195 relying on the 2017 General Plan Update FEIR) with no connection to the shelter. Also identified 5770 Research Drive (matter 19-394) as an unexamined predecessor shelter site.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\legistar2", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T21:05:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-SELF-CORRECTION-LEGISTAR-404", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Retract an incorrect record-availability finding and convert the underlying cause into a tool", "laws": ["Law 16 (Error Coin / AI accountability)", "Law 1 (repeated processes become tools)"], "result": "SELF-REPORTED ERROR, CORRECTED THE SAME SESSION. Task TASK-20261018-ACQUISITION-CHAIN-CORRECTED had asserted as a record-availability defect (OBSTR-HBNC-AVAILABILITY-2026-10-18-16) that two Legistar attachments - MatterAttachmentId 9314 ('Att#4-HA Reso No 7') and 9521 ('Att#2-Affordable Housing Agreement') - returned HTTP 404 from huntingtonbeach.legistar1.com and were therefore not available. THAT FINDING WAS WRONG. Both URLs were re-tested five times each and returned HTTP 200 with full valid content (9314 -> 848,059 bytes, %PDF-, 2 pages; 9521 -> 5,473,870 bytes, %PDF-, 115 pages). The original 404s were TRANSIENT CDN FAILURES. The disconfirming evidence was already in hand and was missed: three other attachments fetched in the same batch (matter 20-1799 attachments 5555, 5576, 5577) also returned 404 stubs and all three succeeded on a simple retry - which should have prompted a retry of the other two before any finding was written. REMEDIATION EXECUTED: (1) OBSTR entry -16 struck in place with a full RETRACTION block, original text preserved rather than deleted; (2) the ledger narrative corrected; (3) both documents captured and added to the package; (4) the true underlying cause - transient 404s from the Legistar CDN - converted into a reusable tool, tools/records_portal/legistar_pull.py, which lists a matter's attachments and downloads them with retry-on-404, %PDF- magic-byte verification, and automatic OCR sidecars. The retained and accurate lesson is operational, not accusatory: any fetch from huntingtonbeach.legistar1.com must retry on 404 and verify the PDF magic bytes, because a reader who trusts a single 404 will wrongly conclude that a published document does not exist. No obstruction of any kind is asserted by this entry.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\OBSTRUCTION_INDICATORS_LOG.md", "supersedes_partial": "TASK-20261018-ACQUISITION-CHAIN-CORRECTED", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T21:30:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-SITE-SELECTION-CHAIN", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Reconstruct the City's full shelter site-selection chain and resolve the 'multiple Navigation Centers' ambiguity", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)", "Law 2 (answer/execute, do not defer)"], "result": "Read the FULL MatterTitle (API, not the truncated agenda display) for every homelessness item in the City's Legistar. The City advanced THREE separate properties under the name 'Navigation Center' within eighteen months. (1) 5770 RESEARCH DRIVE - Legistar 19-394 (MatterId 2369), 2019-04-01, 'Approve and authorize execution of a Three-Year Lease Agreement for 5770 Research Drive and adopt Resolution No. 2019-22 pursuant to SB 850 ... for the Development and Operation of a 50-Bed Navigation Center'. MatterStatusName = WITHDRAWN. Its attachment set included the Ninth Circuit ruling (Martin v. Boise), a Notice of Status Conference, a League of Women Voters letter and a Navigation Center fact sheet - i.e. the item was overtaken by litigation. (2) 15311-15323 PIPELINE LANE - Legistar 19-455 (MatterId 2430), agenda 2019-04-18: 'Adopt Resolution 2019-22 Declaring a Shelter Crisis ... Declare the City's Intent to Proceed with Establishing a Homeless Navigation Center, Authorize Execution of a Purchase and Sale Agreement for 15311 Pipeline Lane, and Amend the FY 2018/19 Revised Budget by $2,850,000'. The executed AIR CRE purchase agreement (attachment 2502) names BUYER: The City of Huntington Beach by ERIK PETERSON, MAYOR; SELLER: WEAVER FAMILY INVESTMENTS, LLC; BROKER: LEE & ASSOCIATES - NEWPORT BEACH (Brad Schneider, Bob Goodmanson; DRE 01887613 / 00595341); form last edited 2019-04-05. That site was bought, then recorded as 'on hold pending litigation' (FY2019/20 CAPER), then sold at a ~$60,000 deficit. (3) 17631 CAMERON LANE / 17642 BEACH BLVD - the only one built; 174 beds; opened the first week of November 2020. OCR CAVEAT RECORDED: only page 9 of the nine-page Pipeline Lane PSA yielded machine-readable text at 200 dpi; the parties and broker are read from the executed acceptance page, which recites them, and the PRICE IS NOT ESTABLISHED by this capture. Also identified a fourth, unrelated property on the same street: 17532 Cameron Lane, 'Cameron Lane Townhomes' (TTM 19154 / CUP 21-006, 18 units, CEQA Notice of Exemption under Guidelines 15195). Dossier section 4E; OBSTR -14 addendum.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_DOSSIER_20261018.md", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T22:00:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-CONTRACT-LISTS-FEMA-AMENDMENTS", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Read the City Clerk's professional-services contract lists for the shelter build and identify every consultancy and amendment", "laws": ["Law 15 (official records, primary evidence)"], "result": "OCR'd and read the two City Clerk quarterly professional-services lists covering the build (Legistar matter 21-256, attachments 1 and 2; Q3 2020 = 2020-07-01 to 2020-09-30, Q4 2020 = 2020-10-01 to 2020-12-31). HEADLINE FINDING - THE FEMA AMENDMENTS: three separate contractors had amendments whose ENTIRE stated purpose, in the City's own words, was federal-reimbursement compliance. (a) 2020-08-27 RPM Team, LLC Amendment #1 - 'Sections added to comply with FEMA Reimbursement Requests' - As Needed Construction Design Management for the COHB Navigation Center; (b) 2020-09-30 Totum Consulting Amendment #1 - 'Sections added to comply with FEMA Reimbursement Requests' - As Needed Construction Project Management for the COHB Navigation Center; (c) 2020-10-19 AESCO Amendment #1 - 'Sections Added to Comply with FEMA Reimbursement Requests' - Testing and Inspection Services for Construction of the New Shelter Facility. All three fall between 2020-08-27 and 2020-10-19, i.e. DURING the construction window and AFTER the Emergency Facility Use Agreement of 2020-04-20 that imported Davis-Bacon and 2 C.F.R. Part 200. This is the contractual counterpart to the EFAU: the City did not merely hope for FEMA money, it amended its own construction consultancies so their costs would qualify. VENDORS ON THE BUILD (all new names in this dossier): RPM TEAM, LLC - nte $27,680 (2020-08-14), Amd #2 +$5,920 to $33,600 (2020-09-29), Amd #3 +$5,800 to $39,400 (2020-09-30); TOTUM CONSULTING - nte $143,000 (2020-08-14), Amd #2 +$99,050 to $242,050 (2020-11-06), Amd #3 +$26,000 to $268,050 (2020-12-01), Amd #4 +$36,000 to $304,050 (2020-12-23); EEC ENVIRONMENTAL - nte $63,000 (2020-08-31), Amd #1 +$75,000 to $138,000 (2020-12-22); AESCO - nte $55,100 (2020-09-29), Amd #2 +$11,800 to $66,800 (2020-12-01). Total professional-services layer approximately $548,000 against a $5,610,000 construction figure. Q18 IS THEREFORE ANSWERED for AESCO and EEC: the site-specific task orders ARE these entries - EEC $63,000 to $138,000 and AESCO $55,100 to $66,800, matching the amounts previously known from the amendment matrix, now with dates, purposes and expiry dates. ADDRESS DEFECT (third instance, now inside a paid contract): the SAME EEC contract is described as '17631 Cameron Lane and 17642 Beach Blvd.' on 2020-08-31 and as '17631 Cameron Lane and 7642 Beach Blvd.' on 2020-12-22. Folded into OBSTR -12 as addendum #2. Still open: the general contractor and the construction contracts themselves - these lists cover consultancies only. Dossier section 4F.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\contract_lists", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T22:00:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-LEGISTAR-PULL-TOOL", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Turn the Legistar retrieval routine into a reusable, retry-hardened tool after a self-detected 404 false positive", "laws": ["Law 1 (repeated processes become tools)", "Law 16 (AI accountability)"], "result": "Wrote tools/records_portal/legistar_pull.py. Features: lists a matter's attachments; prints the FULL MatterTitle (because the display title is truncated and the full title routinely contradicts it); downloads each attachment with retry-on-404 across four attempts with exponential backoff; VERIFIES the %PDF- or PK zip magic bytes before accepting a download, so an HTML error page can never be mistaken for a record; is idempotent (a cached file with valid magic bytes is not re-fetched); records every attempt in MANIFEST.json with http_status, bytes and SHA-256; and can chain into pdf_ocr.sh. Reducing to a tool was forced by a self-detected error: an earlier finding (OBSTR -16) claimed two attachments were unavailable because they returned HTTP 404. They were NOT unavailable - both returned HTTP 200 with full valid PDFs on retry (848,059 bytes and 5,473,870 bytes). The 404s were transient CDN failures, and the disconfirming evidence (three other attachments in the same batch that also 404'd and then succeeded) was already in hand and was missed. See TASK-20261018-SELF-CORRECTION-LEGISTAR-404. Accompanying tool upgrades to pdf_ocr.sh: -r DPI (default 250), -n MAXPAGES, and a .PARTIAL marker written beside any sidecar that was deliberately truncated, so a partial OCR can never be mistaken for a complete one.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\legistar_pull.py", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T22:00:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-OCR-ACCELERATION-PERKS", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Activate the user's student-cloud perks as fast OCR engines after the local tesseract path failed on image-only records", "laws": ["Law 1 (repeated processes become tools)", "Law 2 (answer the question before doing the work)", "Law 4 (YOLO / no permission-seeking)", "Law 5 (zero human action required)", "Law 12 (the AI does the work itself, no handoffs)", "Law 14 (reversible backup before modification)", "Law 15 (official records primacy)"], "result": "The user asked whether their 'student perks vsdev' provided faster OCR and tooling. ANSWER: YES. Recon found the ops were already provisioned but unconnected: (1) Azure for Students is live - az CLI signed in as anthony.dimarcello.student@gmail.com, subscription c7c9b67b-33c7-4201-ada2-ae6c0c42fe7e, tenant 35498253-edeb-4502-b104-4a7748358b5b, state Enabled, Microsoft.CognitiveServices already Registered - yet ZERO Cognitive Services accounts existed, so the credit was unspent; (2) GEMINI_API_KEY + GOOGLE_API_KEY present in Amd949609_keys/.env and the gemini CLI v0.64.0 installed. DELIVERED (two new reusable tools): A) gemini_ocr.py - cloud OCR via Gemini native PDF vision. Key was proven LIVE (HTTP 200, 50 models). Default model gemini-3.8-flash; the retired gemini-2.5-flash returns HTTP 404 'no longer available to new users' and names 3.8-flash as the replacement. PROVEN on the exact document that defeated local OCR: the 38-page image-only Ninth Circuit brief (14.4 MB), which tesseract reduced to NOTHING and which is now a 65,579-byte transcript in ~160s. Also fixed the Windows-CRLF trap: a stray \\r on the key makes curl report 'Malformed input to a URL function'. Added self-healing: a re-run detects [OCR-ERROR pages A-B: ...] blocks and re-requests ONLY those chunks, splicing the repair in place, so a transient 503 'high demand' never forces a full re-OCR (demonstrated: one failed chunk repaired in 41s, 0 errors remaining). B) docintel_ocr.py - Azure AI Document Intelligence, deployed as resource di-hbnc-records (rg rg-osintneoai-westus2, westus, SKU F0 = FREE, 500 pages/mo, endpoint https://di-hbnc-records.cognitiveservices.azure.com/, provisioningState Succeeded). prebuilt-layout with -f markdown preserves TABLES for Law-15 tabular enrichment of SEFA/ACFR financials. Credentials appended to Amd949609_keys/.env after a Law-14 backup. SUBSTANTIVE SOURCE FINDING (from the first two OCRs): the 19-394 'Ninth Circuit ruling' is Martin v. City of Boise, No. 15-35845 (the anti-sit-lie / camping-ordinance opinion) - the legal driver cited in the Navigation Center site-selection record; and City Council Resolution 2019-22 declares a shelter crisis under Government Code section 8698.2 and authorises participation in the Homeless Emergency Aid Program (SB 850 / HEAP), finding ~119 unsheltered persons in Huntington Beach. Also observed: the Azure subscription already carries real OSINT infrastructure - rg-osintneoai-prod with container apps app-osintneoai-prod / app-osintneoai / app-osintneoai-worker, a container registry and two VMs. However those container apps are running the placeholder image mcr.microsoft.com/azuredocs/aci-helloworld:latest with no environment variables, i.e. scaffolding, not working services. A full-corpus Gemini OCR sweep of the FEMA/HBNC tree (74 PDFs) was launched in the background.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\gemini_ocr.py; C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\docintel_ocr.py; C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\site_selection\\2369_2312_AttNo4_Ninth_Circuit_Court_of_Appeals_Ruling.gemini.txt", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T16:59:42.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-GH-TOKEN-AND-MODEL-QUOTA", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Repair stale GitHub CLI auth with the valid stored PAT, and identify the Gemini free-tier model-quota trap that silently poisoned the OCR sweep", "laws": ["Law 3 (zero loss of information / everything completed is recorded)", "Law 4 (autonomous execution, no permission-seeking)", "Law 13 (no stale or hanging background tasks)", "Law 14 (reversible backup before modification)"], "result": "TWO DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED WHILE WIRING THE STUDENT PERKS. (1) GitHub CLI auth was stale: `gh auth status` reported 'The token in default is invalid' for Tonypost949, yet a VALID PAT was already sitting unused in Amd949609_keys/.env as GH_TOKEN (and GITHUB_PAT). Verified directly against the API - login Tonypost949, name 'Osint Neo', x-ratelimit-remaining 4996, scopes include repo, workflow, delete_repo, gist, admin:org. Repaired by logging out the stale account and running `gh auth login --with-token`, so gh now authenticates from ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml without relying on an exported env var. This matters because pushing to GitHub is a mandated backup remote in the OVERRIDE OPTIONS. (2) MODEL-QUOTA TRAP: the first corpus sweep used gemini-3.8-flash (the newest flash, and the model the API itself recommended when gemini-2.5-flash was retired). It worked briefly and then began returning HTTP 429 RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED - 'Quota exceeded for metric: generativelanguage.googleapis.com/generate_content_free_tier_requests, limit: 20' - and the quota was NOT recoverable by retry, because it is a free-tier ceiling on that specific model, not a transient spike. Proven by direct single-request probe of each candidate: gemini-3.8-flash = 429 exhausted; gemini-3.5-flash = 200 OK; gemini-3.1-flash-lite = 200 OK; gemini-2.5-flash-lite = 404 retired; gemini-3.8-flash-lite = 404 not found. FIXES APPLIED: default OCR model changed to gemini-3.5-flash (verified working); a genuine global requests-per-minute limiter (`--rpm`, default 18, threading.Lock + monotonic next-slot scheduling) added to gemini_ocr.py so parallel workers pace themselves instead of trading 200s for 429s; and the sweep relaunched with `-m gemini-3.5-flash --rpm 15`. Post-relaunch observation: 0 rate-limit errors. LESSON: on this key, the newest model name is the WORST choice - always probe quota per model before committing a bulk run, and treat HTTP 429 on a free-tier metric as a model-selection error, not a retry problem.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\gemini_ocr.py; C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\Amd949609_keys\\.env (GH_TOKEN); /home/osintneoai/.config/gh/hosts.yml", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T17:02:43.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-OC2-VM-UPGRADE", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "VM opencode window was still running the old 1.18.34 engine instead of OpenCode 2", "laws": ["Law 1 (repeated processes become tools)", "Law 7 (complaint = immediate end-to-end fix)", "Law 12 (AI does the work itself)", "Law 14 (reversible backup before modification)", "Law 15 (zero data deletion)"], "result": "ROOT CAUSE: two OpenCode installs existed on Kali - the current engine was npm @opencode/cli v2.0.32 at /usr/local/bin/opencode (GEMINI_API_KEY in ~/.bashrc + provider.google.apiKey in ~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc, serve --service on 127.0.0.1:49374), but ~/.bashrc PREPENDED ~/.opencode/bin to PATH, so 'opencode' resolved to the stale v1.18.34 binary - and that stale binary was the one copied to the VM and launched in tmux. FIXED END-TO-END: (1) backed up .bashrc + task files to backups/opencode_bashrc_oc2fix_20261018_170228 (PC) and .bashrc.bak_oc2fix_* on VM; (2) rsynced the v2.0.32 engine package (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/@opencode/cli, node_modules platform variants excluded) to the VM + /usr/local/bin/opencode and opencode2 symlinks; (3) installed Node v24.19.0 + npm 11.17.0 on the VM (same as Kali); (4) fixed PATH ordering on BOTH machines (export PATH=$PATH:.../.opencode/bin - appended not prepended) so v2 wins; (5) upgraded tools/sync_opencode_vm.sh to v2 (now syncs engine package + symlinks + PATH guard, launches /usr/local/bin/opencode); (6) fresh sqlite-consistent session DB snapshot rsynced; (7) tmux restarted - remote pane CONFIRMED: 'Build - Gemini 3.7 Flash (low) Google', version tag 2.0.32; (8) Desktop OpenCode VM.bat relaunched. Old v1.18.34 binary left in place unused on both machines (zero deletion).", "evidence": "/home/osintneoai/tools/sync_opencode_vm.sh; C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\backups\\opencode_bashrc_oc2fix_20261018_170228\\", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T17:05:00.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-N1-TITLE-INSTRUMENTS", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "N1 title instruments: downloader bug fixed, 9 attachments recovered, $120,000 ground lease surfaced", "laws": ["Law 1", "Law 12", "Law 14", "Law 15", "Law 16"], "result": "MY OWN BUG, CAUGHT AND FIXED: n1_download.py rejected every real PDF because the magic check tested data[:4] against the 5-byte needle b'%PDF-'; 7 of 9 downloads were discarded as 'bad magic' while the HTTP fetch had actually succeeded. Fixed to data[:5]/read(5). Re-ran: 9 of 9 attachments recovered into C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\N1_TITLE_INSTRUMENT_20261018 (incl. two 7.42 MB .pptx). Triage: 2 had text; 5 needed OCR. FINDING: the 23-580 / 23-522 'Future Development of 17642 Beach Blvd' PowerPoint decks (extracted with new ooxml_extract.py, no third-party deps) state the MOTIVE in the City's own words: 'Large Deposit (in excess of $6.5M) in FY 19/20 would generate Excess Surplus ... Encumber funds within next three fiscal years ... Opportunity to acquire property became available to resolve Excess Surplus situation', plus 'August 2020 & January 2021 - Project Site Acquired' (matching the two deed dates) and 'long-term use of the Site for anything other than a qualifying affordable housing development would require repayment of the LMIHAF account'. FINDING: the 20-1777 email set gives the missing figure - an adjacent-parcel ground lease of eight months at $120,000 ('Pay for the Toxic site and Lease the Toxic property next door for eight months for $120,000'). FINDING: public soil-contamination knowledge in July 2020; council consideration dated February 18, 2020 (= matter 20-1432, the PSA authorisation). OCR-verified withdrawal memo (20-1777 Att 5489): staff withdrew public agenda item 18 'and will instead agendize the matter as a Closed Session real property negotiation discussion'.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\N1_TITLE_INSTRUMENT_20261018\\", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:37:43.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-LOCAL-OCR-ENGINE", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Unlimited local OCR engine (RapidOCR/ONNX) - ends the cloud-quota dependency", "laws": ["Law 1", "Law 12", "Law 15"], "result": "Built because the Gemini free tier is 20 requests/DAY/project/PER MODEL and requests hang. Installed uv (0.13.1; the host had Python 3.14 with NO ensurepip so `python3 -m venv` could not bootstrap pip), created /home/osintneoai/venvs/ocr on CPython 3.12.15, and installed rapidocr + onnxruntime + pypdf + juriscraper. New tool local_ocr.py: no quota, no network, no per-page cost. SPEED LESSON (measured): `pdftoppm -r 300` burned 2+ minutes of CPU on a 17-page scan, because pdfimages -list showed the pages are ALREADY single full-page JPEGs at 200 dpi - so rendering was pointless upsampling AND re-encoding. local_ocr.py now lifts the embedded rasters with `pdfimages -j` first (17 pages in 1.6 s) and only falls back to pdftoppm for vector/multi-image pages; it also stages the PDF to local disk first because /mnt/c (Windows drvfs) is slow on repeat reads. Fixed a numpy truth-value bug ('truth value of an array with more than one element is ambiguous') caused by `getattr(...) or []` on a RapidOCR ndarray. Output uses the same `=== PAGE n ===` markers as gemini_ocr.py so the engines are interchangeable in the dossier.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\local_ocr.py; /home/osintneoai/venvs/ocr/", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:37:43.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-KNABB-FEDERAL-CASE", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Federal RCRA citizen suit recovered: Knabb v. City of Huntington Beach, 8:26-cv-00348-JWH-(ADSx)", "laws": ["Law 15", "Law 11", "Law 12"], "result": "THE single largest addition to the Cameron Lane file. Using CourtListener (Free Law Project) I recovered the Amended Verified Complaint (doc #8, 40 pp, 314,888 B) from RECAP public storage with NO PACER fee. Case: Jesse Knabb v. City of Huntington Beach, 8:26-cv-00348-JWH-(ADSx), C.D. Cal. Southern Division, District Judge John W. Holcomb, referred to Magistrate Autumn D. Spaeth; original filed 2026-02-14, amended 2026-03-25; nature of suit 893 Environmental Matters; cause 42:6901 = RCRA citizen suit. PLAINTIFF: Jesse Knabb, in pro per, a former Navigation Center resident (Nov 2022-Oct 2024). DEFENDANTS: City of Huntington Beach, OCHCA, RWQCB Santa Ana Region 8, EEC Environmental, Mercy House Living Centers, Inc., Mitsuru Yamada, Does 1-100 - i.e. THIS DOSSIER'S OWN CAST. Key dated allegations: para 37 deed for 17631 Cameron Lane transferred from the Yamada Living Trust to the City 2020-04-03, acceptance delayed to 2020-08-14, recorded 2020-08-19 (a 4.5-month gap); para 38 RAS forms filed 2020-04-27 signed by GEORGE FELIX, a CBRE real estate broker, as 'Person or Entity Responsible for Cleanup'; para 39 OCHCA acceptance 2020-05-04 and OCHCA Case No. 20IC002; para 40 2020-04-30 OCHCA+RWQCB bypassed DTSC by calling it a 'simple waste cleanup'; para 41 2020-07-07 OCHCA PM TAMARA ESCOBEDO ordered parcels merged to avoid parcel-specific DTSC review; paras 42-44 dual GeoTracker IDs, second under the non-existent agency 'Orange County', CalEnviroStor T10000020555; para 36 hexavalent chromium 980 ug/kg ~49x health-protective thresholds plus lead, arsenic, toxaphene, 4,4'-DDE; para 35 Well W-4150 (State Well No. 05S/11W-25M09) and a 1990 Yamada-signed cesspool backfill permit; para 34 MARY ADAMS URASHIMA CEQA objection 2020-08-01 and the '3CS' historic designation from the City's 2014 Historic Context & Survey Report. CAUTION RECORDED: it is a COMPLAINT - an allegation, not a finding; no defendant has answered. Used as a roadmap to primary records (RAS forms, OCHCA 20IC002, GeoTracker T10000020555, DTSC referral, 1990 well permit). Exhibits 1-19 are NOT in RECAP - only doc #8 is archived. Independently corroborated already: the 1947 structure + permit B2020-004554 + HRB reviewer Kathie Schey, and OCHCA 20IC002. Also surfaced docket metadata for three same-judge matters (SEC v. Slaga 8:23-cv-01425, US v. Slaga 8:23-cr-00108, US v. Sidhu 8:23-cr-00114) - recorded as OFFICIAL DOCKET METADATA ONLY, with an explicit caution that no link to Cameron Lane is established and the shared judge is a scheduling fact, not evidence.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\federal_court\\KNABB_v_HB_CACD_8-26-cv-00348\\", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:37:43.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-RECAP-SWEEPER", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "recap_pull.py - token-free RECAP docket sweeper for federal pleadings", "laws": ["Law 1", "Law 12"], "result": "CourtListener's REST API needs a token (docket detail + docket-entries both return HTTP 401 anonymously) and its HTML returns 403 to a non-browser User-Agent, BUT the RECAP *documents* themselves are served from public object storage with no auth at a predictable path: https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.<court>.<pacer_case_id>/gov.uscourts.<court>.<pacer_case_id>.<doc>.<att>.pdf So a docket can be swept by probing document numbers and keeping whatever returns a real PDF - recovering the primary pleading set with no PACER account and no per-page fees. Wrote recap_pull.py (--court --case-id --out --docs --attachments --threads --docket). BUG CAUGHT IN MY OWN TOOL: the first version dropped the case-id-named subdirectory from the storage URL and found 0 documents while a manual curl of the same document returned HTTP 200; fixed and re-ran -> doc 8.0 recovered. Verified pattern: /recap/<stem>/<stem>.<doc>.<att>.pdf. Also archives the public docket HTML. Lesson banked: when a probe loop finds nothing but a hand-check succeeds, suspect the URL BUILDER, not the target.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\recap_pull.py", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:37:43.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-OOXML-EXTRACT", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "ooxml_extract.py - text from PPTX/DOCX/XLSX with no third-party dependencies", "laws": ["Law 1"], "result": "Legistar attachments for the 23-522 / 23-580 'Future Development of 17642 Beach Blvd' items are PowerPoint files; python-pptx was not installed. A .pptx is just a zip of XML, so ooxml_extract.py pulls the <a:t> text runs in slide order, plus speaker notes, and also handles word/document*.xml (DOCX) and xl/worksheets/sheet*.xml (XLSX). Extracted both decks (4,057 and 3,526 chars) and it is where the City's own statement of motive for the acquisition was found. Stdlib only - zipfile + re.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\tools\\records_portal\\ooxml_extract.py", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:37:43.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-GEOTRACKER-OFFICIAL", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Official state register pulled: GeoTracker T10000020555 - site opened AND closed the same day, no contaminants recorded", "laws": ["Law 15", "Law 11", "Law 16"], "result": "Pursued the official record rather than the allegation, per Law 15. GeoTracker and EnviroStor web front ends are both Cloudflare-gated ('Just a moment...') and their ArcGIS REST route is Incapsula-protected, so instead of fighting the gate I took the OPEN DATA route: data.ca.gov's CKAN API (no gate) exposed the Water Boards' full GeoTracker site registry as a CSV. Downloaded geotracker_sites_2026-10-01.csv - 72,945,547 bytes, 89,511 site rows - and searched it. FOUND: GLOBAL_ID T10000020555, BUSINESS_NAME '17631 CAMERON LANE AND 17642 BEACH BLVD', STREET_NAME 'CAMERON AND BEACH LN', HUNTINGTON BEACH CA 92647, lat/long 33.7067073/-117.9880459, LOC_CASE_NUMBER 20IC002, LEAD_AGENCY 'ORANGE COUNTY LOP', CASE_TYPE Cleanup Program Site, STATUS 'Completed - Case Closed', BEGIN_DATE 2020-08-21, STATUS_DATE 2020-08-21, NO_FURTHER_ACTION_DATE 2020-08-21, and BOTH POTENTIAL_CONTAMINANTS_OF_CONCERN and POTENTIAL_MEDIA_OF_CONCERN = 'NA'. THIS SETTLES THREE THINGS: (1) the two-parcel identity (17631 Cameron Lane AND 17642 Beach Blvd in one state case) is the regulator's own, matching the federal complaint's dual-parcel listing paras 42-44; (2) 20IC002 is confirmed by the state, not merely by a party; (3) the lead agency is the County LOP, NOT DTSC - the structural fact behind the alleged DTSC-referral bypass. TWO ANOMALIES: the case OPENS AND CLOSES ON THE SAME DAY (2020-08-21), and that day is TWO DAYS AFTER the deed recorded (2020-08-19 per complaint para 37); and the register records NO contaminants of concern - although the same case file contains an OCHCA letter of 2020-05-22 listing hexavalent chromium 980 ug/kg v 300, 4,4'-DDE 3,100 v 2,000, toxaphene 1,600 v 450 and lead 101 v 80. Logged as a DATED RECORD-INTEGRITY DEFECT with intent UNVERIFIED: OBSTR-HBNC-REGISTER-2026-10-18-17, including the benign explanations (partial register entry for the case type; begin-date convention on a local-oversight closure; analytes held in documents not the site row per the register's own FILE_LOCATION note). Written up in state_regulators/GEOTRACKER_OFFICIAL_RECORD_T10000020555.md and folded into the master dossier as section 4I.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\state_regulators\\", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:46:30.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-LUNA-REYNOSA-2020", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "City's own 2020-08-03 briefing yields the motive, the price, the official contaminant table and the funding plan", "laws": ["Law 15", "Law 12", "Law 16"], "result": "OCR'd matter 20-1799's attachment 3773_5576 (Ursula Luna-Reynosa, 'Cameron/Beach Site - Establishing An Operational 174-Bed Shelter Facility By November 2020', 23 slides) with the local engine, then read it. FIVE MAJOR FINDINGS. (1) MOTIVE FOR THE TWO-PARCEL ASSEMBLY: slide 6 - Judge David O. Carter's Martin v. City of Boise interpretation allowed enforcement only where beds existed for 60% of the unsheltered population, 'as many as 174 beds per night in HB'. Slide 10 reproduces a 2020-07-30 email from Jim Smith (jsmith@jamsadr.com, JAMS) to City Manager Oliver Chi warning that a '+-40 low barrier shelter beds' plan would draw an immediate TRO and that the real threshold was 'not less than 179 beds'; slide 9 gives 289 unsheltered x 60% = 174. Slide 11: the Cameron site ALONE yields only ~75 beds, so staff 'worked out arrangements with the County to expand the shelter onto both the Cameron and Beach parcels'. The adjacent Beach parcel was ARITHMETICALLY REQUIRED, not a convenience - a non-environmental motive to move fast. (2) THE PRICE, on the City's own record (slide 16): 'a credit against the current Cameron site purchase price of $3.2 M'; 'an existing option to purchase the Beach site for $3.2 M'; and authority 'not to exceed $120,000' for a ground lease of the Beach parcel - which CONFIRMS from the City side the $120,000 figure previously resting only on a resident's public comment. Funding source stated: 'Affordable housing dollars (former RDA funds)' = LMIHAF. AND THE SPLIT-CLOSING MOTIVE IS STATED VERBATIM: 'for tax purposes, the property owners do not want to close escrow on the Beach site until 2021 at the earliest' - the documented explanation for the two acquisition dates in the City's own 23-580 deck. (3) THE OFFICIAL CONTAMINANT TABLE: slide 14 reproduces the OCHCA letter of 2020-05-22, Case 20IC002, signed Anthony F. Martinez CEG #2255 (Director Clayton Chau; Assistant Director Lilly Simmering; CC David Ahern OC Public Works and David Bernier EEC), listing hexavalent chromium 980 ug/kg v DTSC HERO Note 3r 300, 4,4 DDE 3,100 v 2,000, toxaphene 1,600 v 450, lead 101 v 80 - i.e. the SAME 980 ug/kg the federal complaint alleges, independently reproduced in the CITY'S OWN COUNCIL FILE. Remedy stated: an asphalt cover; letter expressly conditional ('Soil contamination however, must be addressed if changes to site use occur'). Site described as a 0.79-acre unpaved lot; order from the Governor's Executive Order of 2020-03-18. (4) ENVIRONMENTAL CHRONOLOGY (slide 13): EEC engaged 2020-03-18 for a Phase I as acquisition due diligence; additional sampling at 6 locations; OCHCA selected as oversight agency because of 'the need to perform environmental remediation'; random sampling May 2020 on both parcels; Beach-site testing only 'last week' (late July 2020). Slide 8 lists 'Environmental testing / mitigation for the Cameron site' as a DELAY factor and slide 15 is titled 'Construction Timeline Driving Property Negotiation Process'. (5) FUNDING (slide 17): $4.2 M restricted COVID-19 funds, itemised as Shelter Site Development $2,220,000 + Mercy House Operations $550,000 + Beach Ground Lease $120,000 + FEMA Reimbursement Match $1,185,214; and slide 11 states the COUNTY 'will be performing all of the on-site, utility, shelter installation, and associated development costs'. ALSO NEW: the site-selection list (slide 7) adds two never-examined candidates, 17712 Crabb Lane (Jul 2019) and 17371 Gothard Street / City Corporation Yard (Dec 2019), and writes the site as '17361 Cameron Lane' - the transposed variant again, this time in a council presentation. Reconciliation items opened: $6.4 M nominal v $5,863,000 LMIHAF v the negotiated 'credit'; and $2,220,000 City site development v the $5,610,000 construction figure given the build was County-executed. Written up in site_selection/ANALYSIS_LUNA_REYNOSA_2020-08-03.md and folded into the master dossier as section 4J.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\site_selection\\ANALYSIS_LUNA_REYNOSA_2020-08-03.md", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:46:30.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-OBSTR-17", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "OBSTR-17 logged: register-level record-integrity defect (same-day open/close, empty contaminant fields)", "laws": ["Law 15", "Law 16"], "result": "Added entry OBSTR-HBNC-REGISTER-2026-10-18-17 to OBSTRUCTION_INDICATORS_LOG.md. Classification: RECORD-INTEGRITY DEFECT, dated by the State itself, intent UNVERIFIED. Records the register row verbatim, the contradiction against OCHCA's own 2020-05-22 letter, a full dated sequence table (2020-03-18 EEC engagement through 2020-08-21 closure), and FOUR benign explanations that must be excluded before intent is alleged (partial register entry; begin-date convention on local-oversight closures; analytes held in documents per the register's own FILE_LOCATION note; and the ordinary character of an asphalt-cap conditional approval). Logged anyway because the register is what an ordinary reader or reviewing agency consults, and on its face it shows a completed, closed cleanup case for two parcels with an EMPTY contaminant field, closed two days after the deed recorded, in a sequence in which the City controlled the environmental review. This is the first entry in the log to document a defect in a record CREATED BY A REGULATOR (rather than friction against the Relator's own access), and it is kept strictly separate from the retracted availability claims - OBSTR-16 remains struck.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\OBSTRUCTION_INDICATORS_LOG.md", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:46:30.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-LOCAL-OCR-SWEEP", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Local OCR sweep completed: 16 documents, ~269,000 chars, ZERO errors, no quota spent", "laws": ["Law 1", "Law 12", "Law 15"], "result": "The rewritten local_ocr.py swept 16 previously unreadable image-only PDFs end-to-end with 12/12 and 4/4 success and ZERO LOCAL-OCR-ERROR markers: total ~269 KB of new machine-readable text from documents that had been unreadable. Longest job was the 13-page Research Drive lease at 426 s; the 23-slide Luna-Reynosa deck took 231 s; the 17-page July-2020 email set took 184 s. The pdfimages fast path is what made this possible - pdftoppm had been burning 2+ minutes of CPU on a single 17-page document and emitting nothing. This run is the proof that the cloud-quota problem is solved: 100% of this text came from a local, offline, unlimited engine. Documents recovered include the July 2020 public-comment email sets, the 19-394 Navigation Center fact sheet, the notice of status conference, the 19-455 Pipeline Lane PSA, Resolution 2019-22, Martin v. City of Boise, the OCHCA-vectored Luna-Reynosa deck and memo, and the final site plan.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\site_selection\\", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:05:33.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-CEQA-Q7", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Q7 answered: the City's own written CEQA exemption claim (matter 20-1799 memo, 2020-08-03)", "laws": ["Law 15"], "result": "Matter 20-1799 attachment 5577 is an Inter Office Communication from City Manager Oliver Chi to the City Council dated 3 August 2020 (CC City Attorney Michael Gates, City Clerk Robin Estanislau, Executive Team) and it records how the City disposed of CEQA. It stacks three exemptions: (a) the shelter as a MINISTERIAL use, permitted by right, under Government Code sections 65660-65668 (low barrier navigation center statutes), with land actions declared 'not subject to CEQA' under 65660(b); (b) the common-sense exemption, CEQA Guidelines section 15061(b)(3) - 'it can be seen with certainty that the acquisition will not have a significant effect'; and (c) a DEFERRED affordable-housing exemption under sections 15192/15194, with the analysis expressly postponed 'once an application is submitted and a project description is defined'. NOTE THE COLLISION: the federal complaint para 34 alleges that on 2020-08-01 (two days earlier) historian Mary Adams Urashima objected in writing that the property carried a '3CS' designation in the City's own 2014 Historic Context & Survey Report, that it 'appears eligible for the California Register' and is a historic resource under CEQA, and that no categorical exemption applied. The City's memo asserts exemption on ministerial-land-act and 'certainty' grounds WITHOUT engaging the historic-resource question at all. CAUTION RECORDED: this does not by itself prove a CEQA violation - the ministerial theory under 65660-65668 is a real statutory basis and the deferral language is normal where no project application has been filed. What is now documentary fact is narrower and firmer: the City asserted a ministerial/exempt posture in writing on 2020-08-03 and deferred any environmental analysis without addressing the objection recorded a day earlier. Also captured from the same package: the FINAL ULTIMATE SITE PLAN (7/29/2020, Public Works) showing a '60x194 EXPANDED SPRUNG STRUCTURE (175 BEDS)', a 'STORMWATER DETENTION SYSTEM' drawn on the approved plan (the very system the federal complaint asks the court to suspend), and 'RESTROOM/SHOWER TRAILER AREA (DETAILS BY COUNTY)' - the drawing itself attributing the build to the County.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_DOSSIER_20261018.md", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:05:33.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-PIPELINE-LANE-PRICE", "status": "done", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "Q25/Q26: Pipeline Lane price established at $2,744,000 - and it shares an escrow holder with Cameron Lane", "laws": ["Law 15", "Law 11"], "result": "OCR'd matter 19-455 attachment 2 (the Pipeline Lane purchase and sale agreement, AIR CRE form OFA-20.11, dated 4 April 2019) and read out the terms. THE CONTRACT PRICE, previously unestablished, IS $2,744,000.00. Property '15311-15323 Pipeline, Huntington Beach, CA 92649', APN 145-537-06, buyer THE CITY OF HUNTINGTON BEACH. CORRECTION TO THE FILE: the $2,850,000 figure previously carried was the FY2018/19 BUDGET AMENDMENT, not the price - the ~$106,000 difference must be explained by closing costs, credits or budget margin and must not be reported as the purchase price. Additional deposit $100,000. The deal was SELLER-FINANCED: the City gave a Purchase Money Deed of Trust back to the seller, alongside existing first and second notes. TWO STRUCTURAL FINDINGS: (1) the ESCROW HOLDER is Commonwealth Land Title Company (Grace Kim), 4100 Newport Place - the SAME escrow holder as the Cameron Lane / Beach Boulevard thread, which turns Q22 (the Cameron escrow closing statement) into a nameable target rather than a generic records request; and (2) the brokers are LEE & ASSOCIATES-NEWPORT BEACH (Brad Schneider / Bob Goodmanson) appearing in BOTH the buyer's and seller's blocks with the form's 'dual agent' language engaged - a second, independent dual-agency brokerage thread alongside CBRE on the Cameron side (complaint para 38, George Felix, and Civil Code section 2079.16). Still open and logged: reconcile $2,744,000 against the recorded ~$60,000 loss on later disposition; reconcile the $2,850,000 budget to the price; identify the litigation behind the 'on hold pending litigation' status (the PSA is silent on it); and confirm the seller of record, which is not legible in this scan (Weaver Family Investments, LLC per the earlier finding). METHOD NOTE: this is a 400-dpi 1-bit grey scan, so the transcript is noisier than the 200-dpi colour documents; the price was cross-read in three places and all three agree. Full write-up in site_selection/ANALYSIS_PIPELINE_LANE_PSA.md.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\hbnc_records\\FEMA_PA_AND_FUNDING_20261018\\site_selection\\ANALYSIS_PIPELINE_LANE_PSA.md", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:05:33.000000Z"}
{"id": "TASK-20261018-CALEPA-NSITE-API", "status": "partial", "date": "2026-10-18", "title": "CalEPA nSITE portal API mapped (documents still blocked) - recorded so the route is not rediscovered", "laws": ["Law 3", "Law 12"], "result": "While the GeoTracker and EnviroStor web front ends are Cloudflare-gated and their ArcGIS REST route is Incapsula-protected, I found an UN-GATED aggregation portal: siteportal.calepa.ca.gov/nsite ('nSITE Explorer by Windsor Solutions'), which aggregates GeoTracker/EnviroStor/CERS data. Mapped its API surface from the JS bundle so this is not lost: the client is window.nSITE.Api with baseUrl <origin>/nsite/api; route constants are Site.GetSite='/site' (called as GET /site?SourceIdentifier=<x>), Site.Search='/site/sitecluster', Site.ClaimSite='/site/claimsite', Search.Suggest='/search/suggest'; criteria keys are PascalCase - Keyword, Skip, Take, Zoom, Extent, SourceIdentifier - and the client always serialises them to a QUERY STRING (the 'search' method calls the underlying 'get', so POST is wrong). RESULTS: /search/suggest returns HTTP 200 with a real JSON array (body ignored the param name under every spelling tried and returned []), proving the API is live; /site?SourceIdentifier=<id> returns HTTP 204 No Content for T10000020555, 20IC002 and the full business name, so the SourceIdentifier is a portal-internal key I have not yet derived; and /site/sitecluster returns HTTP 500 'Index was outside the bounds of the array' regardless of Keyword/Skip/Take/Zoom/Extent combinations, which is a server-side fault or a strictly-required parameter set I have not identified. STOPPED HERE by choice: the single highest-value item - the official register row - had already been secured from the open-data export, so continuing to fuzz an unauthenticated API was poor use of time. Recorded as a live route with a known-good base URL for a later pass. The document set behind T10000020555 (RAS forms, boring logs, the 2020-05-22 closure letter) remains the highest-value outstanding primary record.", "evidence": "C:\\Amd949609_Antigravity_v1\\evidence\\state_regulators\\", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:05:33.000000Z"}

